WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.350
Steve Kaufmann here today, I want
to talk about, uh, Elon Musk's,

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.010
Neuralink and the future of language
or the future of language learning.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:14.820
Uh, so, uh, I have done a few videos here
on ChatGPT and Artificial Intelligence,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:20.280
and someone brought to my attention
this issue of Elon Musk's Neurolink.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.520
So I did a bit of
research on the internet.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.690
So my, my views on it are a little
preliminary because I don't know enough,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.820
but I'd be interested in people's
reactions, reaction from people who

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.410
know a lot more about it than I do.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.630
Um, for those of you not familiar
with it, I, I hope I do justice in

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:46.080
explaining the concept, but basically
what Neuralink is, is a chip that will

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:53.129
be implanted in a person's brain or in
a animal's brain, and it is able to sort

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:55.080
of connect with that person's brain.

00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:01.320
It can, uh, sort of anticipate
what the person is going to do,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.040
or at least what they have....

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.970
The experiments that they have done
with animals like monkeys and pigs

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:14.700
show that if a pig or a monkey is
used to doing something, then that

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.244
desire to do something in the brain
is already, sort of, call it signals

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:29.715
amongst the neurons, which can be read
and in some way the, with this chip,

00:01:29.745 --> 00:01:34.905
it's possible to communicate with
those sort of intentions in the brain.

00:01:34.905 --> 00:01:38.175
Either to record them and
possibly to influence them.

00:01:39.005 --> 00:01:44.775
And the idea or the sort of, at this
point we only have experiments of, say,

00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:53.039
a monkey with this chip implant that is
playing pong and is used to controlling

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.240
the activity of pong where, you know, this
little ping pong ball goes up and down

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.520
on a screen, doing it with a joystick.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.170
And when they disconnect the joystick,
the mere fact that the monkey wants

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.289
to move the, you know, items on the
screen a certain way that that is

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.560
enough that he can transmit that
intention to the computer and the

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.670
computer will be basically moving
based on the intentions of the monkey.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.550
The monkey thinks he's controlling
it with his joystick, but the

00:02:20.555 --> 00:02:22.080
joystick has been disconnected.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.670
So that's an example of how the monkey
is communicating to a computer through

00:02:26.675 --> 00:02:30.930
this chip, uh, they showed the pig
where they were able to anticipate

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.830
how the muscles of the pig were going
to move based on the muscle, the

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.420
pig wanted to move a certain way.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.829
And they were able to collect that
information from the chip and see it on

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:46.249
a computer and basically predict which
muscles were going to move on the pig.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:52.920
So those were examples of, call them,
intentions, uh, in the brain, wanting to

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.730
do something that were transmitted out
to a computer and read by the computer.

00:02:57.285 --> 00:03:01.125
Uh, I haven't seen any example of
the computer telling their brain

00:03:01.125 --> 00:03:02.715
what to do, but maybe those exist.

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:03.645
I just haven't seen them.

00:03:04.425 --> 00:03:10.845
Uh, the purpose of this initially was
to say, you know, there are so many

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:14.685
people, for example, who have limited,
limited control of their limbs.

00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:18.795
Or maybe we have Alzheimer's, or we
have Parkinson's, or we have any number

00:03:18.855 --> 00:03:26.985
of neural like brain afflictions that
the hope is that, uh, this chip can

00:03:26.985 --> 00:03:32.595
somehow alleviate those problems,
uh, once we have this communication

00:03:32.595 --> 00:03:37.845
going with this chip going with the
brain, uh, years and years away.

00:03:37.845 --> 00:03:40.935
But that's kind of the
optimistic scenario.

00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:45.885
Uh, it should also be pointed out, at
least in these videos that I saw it said

00:03:45.885 --> 00:03:49.650
that the idea that you can connect, you
know, plant a chip in, in an animal's

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:53.790
brain and follow what it's doing in
this way, is not new to Elon Musk.

00:03:53.795 --> 00:03:55.890
This has been around for 10, 20 years.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.420
What's significant with Elon Musk is
A, he's saying this can help people

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:06.480
who have problems with the brain,
but B, he sort of says that this will

00:04:06.485 --> 00:04:08.550
make it unnecessary to have language.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.730
That language is an
inefficient way to communicate.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:20.040
uh, that once we have these chips, then
people like I could, I would be able to

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.100
communicate from this chip to a device.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.630
The device would then communicate to
another chip in another person, and

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.530
we could be communicating images and
thoughts and concepts without having

00:04:31.535 --> 00:04:35.400
to go through, uh, words or language.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.500
So, and therefore it's not only
language learning that would be

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.550
obsolete, but language would be
obsolete according to this image.

00:04:44.555 --> 00:04:51.390
So whatever, 10, 20, 30 years into
the future, my initial reaction is I

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:58.190
find that language is very useful for
sort of forming your thoughts and.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.270
Uh, I'm not used to the idea
of having concepts that aren't

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.620
expressable in language.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.310
Maybe that's true.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.570
Maybe I'm just old-fashioned.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:12.690
But, uh, the process of expressing my
ideas and thought actually is, is, is fun.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.560
It's quite rewarding.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.930
It's part of what makes us human beings.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:22.780
Uh, I enjoy listening to other people
explain things I enjoy, either challenging

00:05:22.785 --> 00:05:26.950
what they're saying or trying to learn
and, and add on to what they're saying.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.900
And all of this happens in
language, leaving aside whether

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.469
we record it, you know, into audio
or whether we write it down and

00:05:34.469 --> 00:05:36.600
record it by, you know, in a script.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.730
It, it, it's still language.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.880
And I, I think language is something
that we enjoy sharing with other people.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:49.050
Uh, there was, I think in some of
it they said, well, you know, if you

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.840
have this image, you can share this
image with others and then, then

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:52.830
you wouldn't have to describe it.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.500
Not everything we want
to share is an image.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.290
If we wanted to share an
image, uh, I share an image.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:05:59.610
I can do that very easily.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.250
I just, here on my computer,
here's some images.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.130
Last time we were in whatever
country, here's what it was like.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.330
We can share those images.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:10.830
So this nothing, we don't need a
chip planted in our brain to share

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.200
image, uh, if we're sharing ideas.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.510
That's basically a social activity.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.500
Uh, I enjoy making these videos.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.555
I enjoy telling you what I'm thinking.

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:23.925
I enjoy getting the feedback back.

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:27.225
I enjoy the idea that people are actually
listening to me and thinking about

00:06:27.225 --> 00:06:31.035
what I have to say using the language.

00:06:31.065 --> 00:06:33.185
It's, again, it's like learning languages.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.295
As I say, the process is the reward.

00:06:35.745 --> 00:06:39.375
It's all about the enjoyment of,
of sharing things through language.

00:06:40.455 --> 00:06:44.030
I'm not convinced that, uh, we
will do away with the desire

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.235
to  speak uh, and to use language.

00:06:47.295 --> 00:06:53.325
It's possible that, uh, again, maybe
with this chip we can all of a sudden

00:06:53.325 --> 00:06:59.865
input, you know, 50,000 words of Chinese
or the ability to write 4,000 Chinese

00:06:59.865 --> 00:07:04.815
characters can just be quickly dropped
in there or inputted to this chip.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:05.665
It's possible.

00:07:05.745 --> 00:07:07.845
I have trouble imagining
that, but it's possible.

00:07:08.595 --> 00:07:12.351
And yet to me, the process of learning
the language is part of the enjoyment.

00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:16.349
The process of, you know, listening
to a history book, audio book, or

00:07:16.349 --> 00:07:19.890
reading a history book on the country
about, you know, the language that I'm

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.349
starting to explore is interesting to me.

00:07:22.409 --> 00:07:30.659
It's possible that if I were able to buy,
you know, a language, call it Swahili

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.789
or, you know, Urdu, and bingo, I'm
able to communicate in that language.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.050
I might go for that, I don't know, but
I feel a bit uneasy about implanting

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.914
